  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\n   We aim to break the current 0.38085 barrier by introducing a novel cost function that captures the geometry of shift overlap more effectively. The current approach uses a single maxima operation on a convolution, which may not penalize large overlaps sufficiently in directional shifts. Our method introduces a vectorized cost function that considers overlap geometry across multiple scales, effectively regularizing the optimization landscape to avoid high-resolution noise and favor solutions with meaningful spread. The approach relies on resampling at adaptive grid resolutions and coordinate-wise optimization, which allows us to escape local optima more effectively while maintaining sample representation accuracy.\n\n   We identify two key limitations in the previous approach: (a) the max operation creates a non-smooth optimization landscape, and (b) local search operators struggle with maintaining shift invariance. Our solution addresses these by introducing a shifted exponential cost and a hierarchical search strategy that first identifies promising regions through broad optimization and then refines solutions at multiple discrete scales.\n\n   We expect to improve by (a) capturing directional shift behavior accurately, (b) enabling more effective global exploration, and (c) penalizing solutions that concentrate mass near discontinuities. The method is justified by theoretical analysis of optimal shift-aware tight frames, though full proof is omitted here.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n   Variables: h \u2208 [0,1]^n, \u2211h = n/2\n   Objective: C_5 = max_{k=0}^{2n-2} |\u27e8h, \u03c4_k(h)\u27e9| where \u03c4_k is shift operator\n   New formulation: minimize \u03b3 subject to |\u27e8h,\u03c4_k(h)\u27e9|/dx \u2264 \u03b3-\u03b4_k for all k (note \u03b4_k depends on local shift geometry)\n\n   The new cost vector component is:\n     cost_k(h) = |\u27e8h,\u03c4_k(h)\u27e9|/dx - normalized_shift_penalty\n   where normalization uses median shift gain from nearest neighbors to avoid magnitude differences.\n\n   We introduce resampling coordinates for multi-scale exploration and use an exponential transform to convert max objective to sum for stable calculation. The optimization distinguishes between:\n     - Phase 1: Broad optimization using Adam with exponential cost\n     - Phase 2: Local search on multi-resolution grids (using 10-50 trial resolutions)\n     - Phase 3: Vectorized coordinate optimization with shift-constrained projection\n\n   Constraints are enforced by exact linear programming with:\n     - Box constraints: 0\u2264h[i]\u22641 maintained by clipping\n     - Integral constraint: \u2211h = n/2 by rescaling\n\n   Examples:\n     For gradient computation, use:\n        gradient = d(cost)/dh \u2248 (cost(h+\u03b5) - cost(h-\u03b5))/\u03b5\n     where \u03b5 is small (1e-6) and cost includes normalization.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n   a) Initialize candidate h0 from initial_h_values; also generate 10 random binary solutions with:\n        h_bin = np.random.binomial(1,0.5,n) * (n/2 - np.sum(h_bin)) / (n/2)   [integral conditioning]\n        then-lowpass filter to smooth\n\n   b) For adaptive grid resolution:\n        n_tri = [n // factors + 1 for factors in [(3,4), (5,2), (7,1)]]\n        [if sum(h0) != n_new/2, apply transfer by interpolation]\n\n   c) Coordinate-wise optimization using vectorized conjugate gradient with:\n        loss(h) = max_k(cost_k(h)) - apply_exponential_transform(\u00b7)\n        Stop after 500 steps with tolerance 1e-4\n\n   d) Selection criteria:\n        Store best h with minimal \u03b3 + regularization factor ||\u2207\u03b3||^2\n        Use 100 samples reserves by:\n          cost_vectors = np.vstack([compute_cost(h) for h in solution_pool])\n\n   e) Algorithm parameters:\n        learning_rate = {phase1: 0.1, phase2: 0.05, phase3: 0.02}\n        resampling_interval = np.logspace(1,3,10)\n        gradient_clip_threshold = 1e-2\n        exponential_transform_param \u03bb=0.8\n\n   f) The parametric optimization (phase 3) uses:\n        Objectives for coordinate i: cost_i(h) = max_{j} |\u27e8h[i:] \u00b7 \u03c4_j(h[:])\u27e9|/dx\n        Gradient: \u2202cost_i/\u2202h[i] \u2248 d(cost)/d(h[i]) using directional finite difference\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n   Core configuration:\n     - Use scaled gradient descent (phase 1) with adaptive \u03bb adjustment\n     - Resample to 20-100 test points at new intensification stages\n     - Coordinate-wise updates (phase 3) with L2 regularization on cost function\n\n   Key variations:\n     1) Surface analysis: every 200 iterations, check shift direction singularities and bias by:\n          \u03b3_dir = max( \u03b3_k for k=round(np.linspace(-n, n, 20)) )\n     2) Half-space projection: for improved integral, use mirror projections of unsuccessful candidates\n     3) Randomized SVD projection to lower dimension (n_pca = min(10, int(sqrt(n))))\n\n   Each variation is guided by directional fitness scores:\n\n        fitness(k) = (initial_score - current_score) / (initial_score + 1)\n\n   variations are attempted when fitness(k) > threshold = 0.3\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n   Total time: ~800s total. Breakdown:\n     - Phase1: 300s (5 threads concurrent optimization)\n     - Phase2: 300s (resampling to 10 different grid sizes)\n     - Phase3: 300s (coordinate optimization)\n\n   Feasibility checks:\n     - Always repair h via NaN checks and 0/1 clipping\n     - Cost function evaluation: use try/except with default=large value\n     - Time limits: use np.isfinite within descent and C function\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n   Required checks:\n     - np.all(0<= h <=1)\n     - np.isclose(sum(h)*dx, 1)\n     - cost = max(np.correlate(h,1-h,'full'))*dx < threshold\n\n   Final evaluation:\n     - Full grid shift calculation replacing correlation (for accuracy)\n     - 3 independent cost function calls averaged\n\n   The strategy block must contain all elements and be concrete.",
